<p>From 1906 to his death in 1922, Hungarian novelist Géza Gárdonyi kept a secret journal in a script so inscrutable that it wasn&#8217;t deciphered until 1965. He&#8217;d labeled the work a Tibetan grammar, but in fact it employed a calligraphic code founded in Hungarian using symbols that Gárdonyi had devised himself. In it he recorded his thoughts, observations, and literary plans. It was published in 1974 as <em>Titkosnapló</em> (&#8220;secret diary&#8221;).</p>
